<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zheng Keshuang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1670">1670</a>–<a href="/years/1717">1717</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zheng Keshuang was the last ruler of the Kingdom of Tungning on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a>. Court factions placed him on the throne as a boy after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-jing">Zheng Jing</a> died in 1681. In 1683 the Qing admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-lang">Shi Lang</a> destroyed the Zheng fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a>, and Zheng Keshuang surrendered; Taiwan was then annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, and he lived out his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zheng-keshuang.jpg" alt="Zheng Keshuang" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 鄭克塽彩像（清人绘） · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20191015125805_zpgwv.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zheng Keshuang was born on 13 August 1670 in Chengtian Prefecture, whose administrative centre was Fort Provintia. He was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-jing">Zheng Jing</a>, king of Tungning, by his concubine Lady Huang. While campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in the late 1670s, Zheng Jing named his elder son, Zheng Kezang, heir apparent and married his sons to daughters of his two most trusted officials: Kezang to the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-yonghua">Chen Yonghua</a>, Keshuang to the daughter of Feng Xifan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-yonghua">Chen Yonghua</a> was ousted by his rivals Feng Xifan and Liu Guoxuan and died in 1680. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-jing">Zheng Jing</a>'s death, Feng allied with Liu, Zheng Cong and others, told Queen Dowager Dong that Zheng Kezang was not Zheng Jing's own son, and killed him in a coup. The twelve-year-old Zheng Keshuang was installed as Prince of Yanping; he rewarded the officials who had supported the coup with titles of nobility and gave posthumous titles to his ancestors.</p><p>In 1683 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-lang">Shi Lang</a> with a fleet against Tungning, and Shi Lang defeated Liu Guoxuan at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a>. The court then split between a war faction, led by Zheng Dexiao and others, and a surrender faction led by Feng and Liu. Zheng Keshuang took their advice. On 5 July 1683 Feng ordered Zheng Dexiao to write the surrender, and on 13 August Shi Lang entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and received it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> enrolled Zheng Keshuang in the Plain Yellow Banner and made him hereditary Duke of Hanjun. Some former Tungning units, such as the rattan shield troops, joined the Qing army and fought the Russian Cossacks at Albazin. He died of illness in Beijing. His brother Zheng Kexue was ordered to rebury <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koxinga">Koxinga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-jing">Zheng Jing</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>, the family's ancestral home, but Lady Huang failed to recover the family's property in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, which local officials had taken. His descendants served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">bannermen</a> in Beijing until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> of 1911, then moved back to Anhai and Nan'an in southern Fujian. He appears as an antagonist in Louis Cha's novel The Deer and the Cauldron.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zheng Keshuang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Zheng Jing's death in 1681, court factions placed his young son <b>Zheng Keshuang</b> on the throne.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tungning">Kingdom of Tungning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Taiwan Island, in what is now <a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.77,121&amp;year=1700" aria-label="Taiwan Island on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7774 1288 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zheng Keshuang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8224 1558h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8224 1558h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.77,121&amp;year=1700">What was here in 1700? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
